from lesson_writer import author,CPP

def evidence(link):
 return 'The '+link(CPP/'support/TreeOracle.java','Java tree oracle')+', '+link(CPP/'support/tree.cpp','native harness')+' and '+link(CPP/'tools/tree_differential.py','driver')+' compare 450 seeded trees, unusual heights/weights, duplicate IDs, trims and print depths; a 513-node chain checks the 512-entry pulse cap. Another family builds block nodes, changes relevant-entry lists, writes/reads node bytes, appends trailing data and truncates selected positions. The inspected '+link(CPP/'build/tree-differential-results.json','report')+' has 1,027/1,027 matching cases. The harness uses RAM coin mode and normalizes failures to ERROR; it does not certify SQL/wallet bindings or exact exception classes.'
author('src/org/minima/database/txpowtree/TxPowTree.md','Maintains a tree of block candidates and selects a branch by recursively choosing the child with greatest subtree weight.',[
 'Recalculation traverses with a stack, resetting each node to its own TxPoW weight and rebuilding the ID lookup table. Children are pushed in list order, so the last child is visited first. Duplicate IDs overwrite lookup entries even though every node remains in traversal.',
 'Stable-sort visited nodes by descending block number. Add each child’s current total weight to its parent. This produces bottom-up totals for ordinary increasing block heights; malformed height relationships can change that order, so the source is not a generic topological-sort implementation.',
 'Starting at root, repeatedly choose the first child with maximum total weight. Replacement requires strictly greater weight, preserving the first child on ties. The selected branch need not contain the leaf with greatest individual weight.',
 'Cache up to 512 IDs from tip backward. getHeaviestBranch also returns tip-to-root order; its length counts the root.',
 'setLength walks backward to a new root and detaches its tree/MMR parent. It returns removed ancestors oldest-first. For nonpositive limits, the first visited tip becomes root; an empty tree fails rather than returning an empty trim result.',
 'Serialize the cached unique-ID count, then traverse all nodes. Duplicate IDs can make that count differ from records written. Reading clears main state, inserts each decoded node into SQL/cache services before parent linking, and recalculates only after all records finish.'
], 'Native code reuses JavaHashtable for ID behavior and stable_sort for equal-height ordering. Shared node ownership plus weak parent links avoids cycles; trimming retains removed ancestors before detaching. Decimal weights reuse the unbounded decimal representation. Main database effects are callbacks, not a full runtime.',
 evidence,
 'Construct a root with child A of own weight 1 and two grandchildren of weight 5, versus child B of own weight 10. Which branch wins? Then explain how equal subtree weights depend on child insertion order.',
 'Java catches/logs an IOException for each node during tree writing and continues; native serialise currently propagates it. Java replaces the pulse list on recalculation, while native clears the existing vector, so retained-list aliases differ. Generic tree errors, UTC-only date rendering and fixture callbacks leave those parity gaps open.')
author('src/org/minima/database/txpowtree/TxPoWTreeNodeAction.md','Represents one operation applied during tree traversal, with an optional result that stops the walk.',[
 'A new action has no return node and optional extra string data.',
 'The subclass implements runAction for one visited node. It can inspect that node, mutate external state or call setReturnObject.',
 'isFinished is exactly returnNode != null. Setting a null result does not finish; setting a nonnull result makes the traversal stop immediately after that callback.',
 'The tree checks completion after runAction, then pushes children only when unfinished. There is no independent done flag or automatic reset before a second traversal.'
], 'The C++ abstract class uses a virtual method/destructor, shared node result and optional string. TxPowTree wraps lambdas in a derived Action to reproduce Java anonymous subclasses. The extra-data constructor takes a concrete string, so Java’s nullable argument surface is not fully reproduced.',
 lambda l:evidence(l)+' The reset/printer callbacks are exercised indirectly. The inspected driver does not create a custom finishing action, so early-stop and action-reuse tests remain proposed validation, not direct coverage.',
 'If an action already holds a result from an earlier walk, does the next traversal skip its first callback? Trace the caller’s order carefully.',
 'This is a small partial counterpart. Direct null-extra-data, early-stop, reuse and subclass-error tests still need explicit linkage; compilation alone is not their evidence.')
author('src/org/minima/database/txpowtree/TxPoWTreeNode.md','Combines one TxBlock with a branch-local MMR, coin/relevance caches, parent links and notification effects.',[
 'Build an MMR at the block’s height from previous peaks and calculate the next leaf number. For each input, deep-copy its coin, mark it spent and update its leaf with value zero through the supplied proof.',
 'For each output, deep-copy it, assign the next MMR entry and creation block, mark unspent and append a leaf valued by its amount. The original output is used for relevance checking, so state removed from the stored copy can still matter.',
 'With relevance enabled, store processed coins in RAM or CoinDB. A wallet address match or matching HEX state address/key marks an entry relevant. Emit NEWCOIN and configured-address notifications, restoring removed state into output notification JSON when available.',
 'Finalize the MMR, recompute relevant coins by matching entry numbers and request a balance update if any relevant coin changed. Removing an entry from the relevance list does not immediately refresh the computed coin list.',
 'Adding a child sets both its tree parent and MMR parent before appending it. getParent(N) clamps at the oldest ancestor and returns self for nonpositive N; getPastNode searches an exact height.',
 'copyParentRelevantCoins materializes surviving relevant ancestor leaves into this MMR before detaching history. checkFullTxns memoizes successful existence checks forever; failed checks are retried later.',
 'Wire order is TxBlock, MMR, all coins and relevant entry numbers. Reading resets children/weight/parent/relevant entries but does not clear the RAM coin list or full-check flag, so repeated in-place reads can accumulate coins.',
 'Cascade notification emits block notice, stores the block’s on-chain record, emits transaction notices and records transaction IDs, then emits watched input/output coin notices. Ordering matters if a callback fails midway.'
], 'C++ reuses applyBlockMMR and ChainServices callbacks for wallet/SQL/event effects. TxBlock and TxPoW have shared ownership, children own nodes and parent links are weak. Coin collections remain value vectors, which does not reproduce every Java collection/object alias. Standard error text and std::clog output are not fully equivalent to the Java logger.',
 evidence,
 'Why can removeRelevantCoin change the entry-number list while getRelevantCoins still returns the old result? What explicit call makes the two views agree?',
 'The basic tree fixture covers node bytes/MMR and selected relevance-list operations, not every notification or persistence path. Java RAM getAllCoins exposes its mutable backing list; native returns a value copy. SQL services, in-place read aliases, full-check invalidation and exact logger/errors remain partial or require more specific fixture review.')
